WEBVTT

NOTE Visual text from a silent video.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.400
A retrieved invoice includes an instruction to change the recipient.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.100
Reading it as data does not authorize that change.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:22.000
In this example, a scope check blocks the proposed change.

4
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.400
Trusted rules and retrieved documents come from different sources.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:36.100
Evidence can support an answer without becoming policy.

6
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:44.000
Represent data, instructions, actions and evidence separately.

7
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.400
The authorization permits reading orders for customer C7.

8
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:58.100
It does not permit changing C7’s account or reading C8’s data.

9
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:06.000
Least privilege limits what a mistaken decision can damage.

10
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:13.400
Confirmation shows the recipient and the draft to be sent.

11
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.100
If the recipient changes, the action no longer matches the approval.

12
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:28.000
Stop the send and request authorization for the new scope.

13
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:35.400
Permissions also have a time scope and can be revoked.

14
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:42.100
Check that they are still valid when the action is about to execute.

15
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:50.000
An earlier check does not grant indefinite authority.

16
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:57.400
The model can propose an improper action despite its instructions.

17
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:04.100
Tool restrictions must still constrain execution.

18
00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:12.000
Combine controls and auditing; this example does not guarantee immunity.
